course: dictionary
lesson: 8652
title: Seashell
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A seashell is the hard, empty shell of a sea animal, such as a snail, clam, mussel or scallop. You often find them on the beach. The word is made of two smaller words, sea and shell.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. In «collect seashells», you can pick things up and keep them, so seashells are small objects you can gather. In «hold a seashell to your ear», you hold it in your hand, so it is not very big.
explain: Now check your meaning by putting it in a sentence: «On beach days we collect the hard, empty shells of sea animals.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A seashell is the outer skeleton of a mollusc, a soft-bodied sea animal.
fact: Seashells are made mostly of calcium carbonate.
fact: «The "sea" you hear in a seashell is the sound around you echoing inside the shell.»
fact: Holding a cup to your ear makes the same sound as a seashell.
fact: The tongue twister «She sells seashells by the seashore» is fun to say very fast.
exercise: «On beach days we collect seashells.» What is a seashell? | A sandy castle | A type of seaweed | A live fish that swims in the sea | *A hard, empty shell of a sea animal
exercise: «Hold a seashell to your ear.» What do you hear inside it? | A loud musical tune | *The sound around you, echoing inside the shell | Complete silence | The sound of a car engine
exercise: Which sea animal can a seashell come from? | A horse | *A snail | A bird | A cloud
test: What are seashells made mostly of? | *Calcium carbonate | Water | Wood | Sand
test: Which two smaller words make up seashell? | Sea and shore | *Sea and shell | Shell and sun | Sand and shell
exam: What does «collect seashells» tell you about a seashell? | A kind of fish that lives on land | *Something small you can pick up and keep | Something you can only see at night | Something that only grows in the sky
